Abstract
In this study, binary metal organic frameworks (MOFs) with HKUST-1 and UiO-66 have been synthesized in a one-pot process. The synthesized MOFs were characterized by Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy, X-ray diffraction (XRD), scanning electron microscopy (SEM), $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption, and thermogravimetric analysis (TGA). The mesoporosity and thermal stability of the binary MOFs were higher than those of single HKUST-1 or UiO-66. The synthesized MOF hybrids were then tested for adsorptive removal of methylene blue (MB) from wastewater in terms of kinetic and isothermal adsorption as compared to a commercially available activated carbon (AC). All the synthesized MOFs showed significant removal of MB under a wide range of pH. The adsorption capacities of HKUST-1 are higher than UiO-66 and commercial AC while the binary MOFs presented an even higher adsorption capacity than single MOFs. This is the first time that binary HKUST1 and UiO-66 MOFs have been successfully synthesized and demonstrated enhanced adsorptive removal of contaminants.
Key words: Metal organic frameworks, adsorptive removal, binary MOFs, methylene blue, HKUST-1, UiO-66
## 1. Introduction
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) have emerged as a promising class of porous materials in the last two decades [1]. There is an increasing literature on the synthesis and application of new and existing MOFs with tailored properties. The beauty of MOFs lies in their tuneable textural and chemical properties [2-5]. The high surface area, tuneable pore size, incorporation of various functionalities by in situ and/or post-synthesis treatment make them highly demanding materials in gas adsorption and separation, catalysis, energy storage, supercapacitors, biomedical and wastewater treatment processes [6-10]. Recently, MOFsbased composites with various materials have gained attention [11-16].
In recent years, with the immense industrialization and urbanization, the amount of wastewater has increased sharply while fresh and potable water is continuously decreasing. The wastes discharged from various industries include heavy metals, personal care products, pharmaceuticals, herbicides and dyes. The amount of dyes produced every year is approximately 9 million tons with 100,000 varieties [17, 18] and are usually used in textile, food and coloring industries. These dyes are causing harmful effects on aquatic life and removal of the dyes is vital as some of them are even carcinogenic [19]. Methylene blue has been widely studied in wastewater treatment processes, being one of the common dyes discharged into water bodies [19-23]. There are various methods for dye removal namely, catalytic oxidation, biological degradation and adsorption [24-26].
Adsorption is one of the promising methods for dye removal from water. However, there are various challenges in the removal of dyes including MB from wastewater, e.g. low adsorption capacity of adsorbents, limited operative pH range and regeneration of the materials [7, 27,
28]. Pristine and composite adsorbents, e.g. activated carbons, graphene, graphene oxide, mesoporous carbons, clays, zeolites, activated carbon-metal oxide composites and MOFs have been reported for the adsorptive removal of MB from wastewater [5, 9, 17, 29-33]. However, wastewater treatment processes (WWTPs) using MOFs are very limited due to instability of the MOFs in aqueous media, low adsorption capacity, limited operating range in terms of pH. Enamul et al. [34] reported the first ever MOF application in WWTPs for the removal of methyl orange using Cr-based MOF. It was observed that functionalization of the MOF with $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group resulted in an adsorption capacity of 194 mg/g for methyl orange. Ever since, some other MOFs have been employed in the removal of dyes but limited to a group of MILs, i.e. MIL-53, MIL-100, and MIL-101 [20, 35-38]. The use of Cu-based MOFs (HKUST-1) has gained attention in the last couple of years when a CuBTC was used in the adsorptive removal of MB. However, a very limited MB capacity was achieved [7]. Moreover, electrostatic interaction was reported as the main factor in removal of MB confirmed by the pH effect. In another study, terphenyl tetracarboxylic acid $\mathrm{\cdotH_{4}T P T C)}$ was used in combination with terphenyl hexa carboxylic acid $\mathrm{\cdotH_{6}T P T C)}$ for MOF synthesis, resulting in a higher adsorption capacity of MB [39]. However, the cost of the linkers for the preparation is very high for implement at a larger scale.
There are some studies on adsorptive removal of MB using Cu-based MOFs and UiO-66 [7, 40, 41]. UiO-66 and HKUST-1 are stable MOFs in aqueous media and have been used in various organic waste removal processes [42-44]. Composites of HKUST-1 with graphite oxide have been reported in ammonia adsorption and MB removal [45, 46]. The synergic effect of zirconium hydroxide composited with HKUST-1 in toxic chemicals (ammonia, cyanogen chloride, and sulfur dioxide) removal by physically mixing with zirconium hydroxide was also tested [13]. However, there are no studies on bimetallic Cu and Zr MOFs in WWTPs. In the present study, binary MOFs were synthesized in situ to make a comparison of their capacity for adsorptive removal of MB. Specifically, carboxylic acid based linkers were selected to reinforce functionalities in composite MOFs with a bi-metallic configuration (Cu and $\mathrm{Zr}$ ). Interestingly, the adsorption of MB on composite MOFs is much higher than the pristine MOFs. This study opens new horizons in terms of in situ synthesis of composite MOFs with synergic effects in WWTPs.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials
Dimethylformamide (DMF, $99\%$ ), benzene-1, 4-dicarboxylic acid (BDC, $98.9\%$ ), $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ $(99.9\%)$ , $\mathrm{Cu(NO_{3})_{2}}{\bullet}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(99\%)$ , benzene tricarboxylic acid (BTC, $95\%$ ), hydrochloric acid (HCl, $36.5\%$ ), sodium hydroxide (NaOH, $97\%$ ), methylene blue (MB, $82\%$ ), and methanol $(99.9\%)$ were supplied by Sigma–Aldrich (Australia). An activated carbon (AC, $98\%$ C) was purchased from Perth Scientific Pty Ltd (Australia) and used without further purification. The ultrapure water (IBIS Alpha, 15.5 mΩ cm, IBIS technology) was obtained from our laboratory.
## 2.2. Synthesis of pristine and composite MOFs
A pristine HKUST-1 was prepared with modification from a reported method [47]. In a typical synthesis, 1.087 g of $\mathrm{Cu(NO_{3})_{2}}{\bullet}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was dissolved in $15~\mathrm{mL}$ absolute ethanol while 0.525 g of BTC was dissolved in another $15~\mathrm{mL}$ absolute ethanol. Then, both solutions were transferred into a Teflon autoclave and heated in an oven at $125~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ The resultant MOF was filtered under vacuum and dried at $155~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight.  The sample was labelled as C.
A pristine $\mathrm{z}_{\mathrm{r}}$ -UiO-66 was synthesized by a reported procedure with slight modification [48]. In a typical synthesis, $1.087\mathrm{~g~ZrCl_{4}}$ and $0.525\mathrm{~g~}$ benzene dicarboxylic acid (BDC) were separately dissolved in a solvent (DMF) of $15~\mathrm{mL}$ . Both solutions were stirred for $15~\mathrm{min}$ , mixed together and then stirred for $20\ \mathrm{min}$ . Finally, the solution was transferred to an autoclave and heated in an oven at $125^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The sample was designated as Z.
In the synthesis of binary metal organic frameworks, $\mathrm{z}_{\mathrm{r}}$ -UiO-66/Cu-HKUST-1(C1), 1.087 g of $\mathrm{Cu(NO_{3})_{2}}{\bullet}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $0.525\ \mathrm{g}$ of BTC were separately dissolved in $15~\mathrm{mL}$ absolute ethanol, mixed and stirred at $300\mathrm{rpm}$ and $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ . Then $0.0544\mathrm{g}\mathrm{Zr}\mathrm{Cl}_{4}$ and 0.0263 g BDC were dissolved in DMF and stirred at $300~\mathrm{rpm}$ and $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Subsequently, the  above two solutions were poured into a Teflon autoclave and heated in an oven at $125^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ .
For the synthesis of Cu-HKUST-1/Zr-UiO-66 (Z1), 1.087 g ZrCl4 and $\phantom{+}0.525\textrm{g}$ BDC were dissolved in $15~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF each and mixed together. Then $0.0544\textrm{g C u}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\bullet}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was dissolved in $0.75{\mathrm{~mL}}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $0.0263\mathrm{~g~}$ BTC was dissolved in $0.75~\mathrm{mL}$ absolute ethanol, mixed and stirred at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Finally, two solutions were poured into a Teflon autoclave and put in an oven at $125^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ .
All the MOFs were filtered under vacuum and dried at $155~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight. Moreover, all the MOFs were activated using methanol for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ . In the activation process, fixed quantity of MOF was soaked in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol and replenished with fresh methanol after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ .
## 2.3. Characterization of the MOFs
FTIR spectra were used to check the functional groups on the organic ligands and coordinated metal atoms. The spectra were scanned with a resolution of $4~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ using an attenuated total reflectance (ATR) technique on a Spectrum 100 FT-IR Spectrometer (Perkin Elmer, USA). SEM analysis (Zeiss NEON 40 EsB CrossBeam, Germany) was conducted to study the morphologies of the pristine and composite MOFs. Thermal stability of MOFs was investigated by a thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) instrument (TGA/DSC1 STARe systemMETTLER TOLEDO, USA). All the MOF samples were loaded into an alumina pan and heated to $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at a rate of $5~{^\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}$ . An air flow rate was maintained at 10 mL/min. X-ray powder diffraction patterns were obtained with $\mathrm{\Delta}X$ -ray diffractometer (D8 Advance-Bruker aXS, Germany) using $\mathrm{Cu}\ K\upalpha$ radiation $(\lambda=1.5406\mathrm{~\AA})$ , at accelerating voltage and current of $40~\mathrm{kV}$ and $40~\mathrm{mA}$ , respectively, for 2θ ranging between $5{-}40^{\circ}$ . The surface area, pore size and pore volume were measured using ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms on a Micromeritics Tristar II3020, USA. The samples were evacuated at $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $4{\cdot}12\mathrm{h}$ prior to the adsorption measurements under high vacuum. The multi-point BET surface area was calculated by ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption in $\mathrm{P}/\mathrm{P}_{0}=0.05\ –0.15$ , single point pore volume was calculated at $\mathrm{P}/\mathrm{P}_{0}=0.99$ and pore size distribution was determined using the Barrett-Joyner-Halenda (BJH) method. The point of zero charge $\mathrm{(pH_{PZC})}$ was determined using an immersion technique [49]. Suspensions of $10~\mathrm{g/L}$ of MOF were put into contact with 0.03 M ${\mathrm{KNO}}_{3}$ solutions adjusted at different pH values of 3-10. The suspensions were aged for 24 h until an equilibrium pH value was reached. The change in $\mathrm{pH}(\left|\Delta\mathrm{pH}\right|)$ during equilibration was calculated and the $\mathsf{p H}$ for PZC was identified as the initial pH with zero or minimum |ΔpH|.
## 2.4. Adsorption experiments
MB concentrations were determined using the maximum absorbance (at $664~\mathrm{nm},$ of the solutions through UV spectra of the solutions obtained by a spectrophotometer (Thermo Spectronic Genesys 20, Thermo Scientific™, USA).
First, the calibration curve was obtained from the spectra of the standard solutions which were prepared in various concentrations $(1{-}10\ \mathrm{ppm})$ at a specific $\mathsf{p H}$ of 7.5 measured by a WP-81 pH-Cond-Salinity meter. The initial $\mathsf{p H}$ of MB solution was adjusted using $0.1\textbf{M}$ HCl and 0.1 M NaOH.
Second, 1000 ppm aqueous stock solution of MB was prepared by dissolving MB in ultrapure water. Different concentrations of MB $(5{-}100~\mathrm{ppm})$ were prepared by further diluting the stock solution with water to study adsorption isotherms.
In all the batch experiments, the MOFs were dried overnight under vacuum at $140~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and kept in a desiccator. A fixed amount of the MOFs was put in the aqueous dye solutions (100 mL) having fixed dye concentrations from 5 to $100\ \mathrm{ppm}$ . The MB solutions $\mathrm{(pH~}7.5)$ containing the adsorbents were mixed well with magnetic stirring and maintained for fixed time ( $10\ \mathrm{min}$ to $12{\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After adsorption, the solution was separated from the adsorbents by a high speed centrifuge for $5~\mathrm{{min}}$ , and the dye concentration was determined by the UV-Vis spectrophotometer and then the amount of dye adsorbed was calculated by the following equation.
$$
Q_{e}={\frac{(C_{0}-C_{e})V}{m_{a d s}}}
$$
Where $Q_{e}$ is amount of dye adsorbed at equilibrium $(\mathrm{mg/g})$ , $C_{0}$ , initial dye concentration $(\mathrm{ppm}),C_{e}$ , equilibrium dye concentration (ppm), V, volume of dye solution used (L) and $m_{a d s}$ , is the mass of adsorbent (g).
## 3. Results and Discussions
## 3.1. Characterization of MOFs
All the samples were characterized for the binding between metal atoms and linkers (BDC and BTC) (Fig. 1). The characteristic peak of HKUST-1 (C) and its composite MOF (C1) at $728~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ is attributed to Cu-O bond confirming metal-linker coordination [7]. Moreover, the peak at $745~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ represents $Z\mathrm{r}$ -O bond in UiO-66 $(Z)$ and its composite MOF (Z1) [50]. The bands at $1550–1630\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ indicate $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ , while the region of $1450{-}1580~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ belongs to $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}$ of aromatic ring. Moreover, the strong band at around $1370~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ also represents $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=C}}$ of benzene [7, 39, 51, 52]. Furthermore, the peak shifting was observed after MB adsorption on MOFs, suggesting the host-guest interaction between organic moieties of the MOFs and organic dye (MB). However, the peak shift on $Z$ and $Z1$ was not as significant as that on C and C1 after MB adsorption (Fig.1). The shifting of the peaks in the range of 1368-1391 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1562{\cdot}1582\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , in particular, reinforces the $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction of benzene rings of the hostguest. The details of adsorption mechanism are given in section 3.4.
The structure of the single and binary MOFs was studied using XRD (Fig. 2). It is clear that the structure of composite MOFs is similar to that of pristine HKUST-1 and UiO-66. The XRD patterns also show the high crystallinity of pure HKUST-1 and UiO-66 MOFs, which have been studied extensively in literature [42, 50, 53]. Moreover, bimetallic MOFs, C1 and Z1, exhibited similar degrees of crystallinity in the XRD pattern to their parent MOFs (HKUST-1 and UiO-66) as previously reported [54]. Furthermore, distinct octahedral shape crystals can be seen from SEM analysis (Fig. 3). Similarly, the crystals of bimetallic HKSUT-1 (C1) are also in an octahedral shape. Moreover, distinct macro-pores can be seen in C1 (Fig. 1 inset). In the case of Z, the particles are almost in a cubic shape in 200 nm range, whereas Z1 is similar to parent UiO-66 [48, 52].
Thermal stability of pristine and composite MOFs was studied by TGA. In Fig. 4, it can be seen clearly that pristine and composite UiO-66 MOFs are more stable than pristine and composite HKUST-1 MOFs. The degradation starts with the removal of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ at around 100- $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and the major weight loss occurs between 300 and $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $\mathbf{C}$ and C1, which is due to the structural decomposition of organic linkers. However, in case of C1 the thermal stability increases to some extent due to a synergetic effect of UiO-66 and the major weight loss occurs at $370^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ [55]. For Z1, the degradation of the MOF occurs with the removal of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and then with the degradation of the structure, but the stability of Z1 is less than that of Z due to incorporation of less stable HKUST-1 (C) into the composite. It is evident from Fig. 4 that $Z$ and Z1 are stable up to the temperature of $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ while $\mathbf{C}$ and C1 are stable up to $325{-}370~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Moreover, the higher residual weight in the composite MOFs is due to the larger amount of two metallic species [14].
The pore size and surface area are important properties for adsorption applications. The synthesized MOFs exhibited high surface area and large pore volume. Pristine HKSUT-1 (C)
and UiO-66 $(Z)$ showed type I isotherm and higher surface area than the composite MOFs (Fig. 5). The main contribution in such a high surface area is due to microspores which become more prominent by activating with methanol resulting in opening the microspores by removing unreacted BDC/BTC and solvent guest molecules [52]. The composite MOFs, C1 and Z1, exhibited type IV isotherm with a H4 and H2 hysteresis, respectively, which may be attributed to the mesoporous structure. However, the surface area and total pore volume decreased in the bimetallic MOFs, which is similar to the earlier reports [56, 57]. It has been already mentioned that the mixed metal MOFs are accompanied with the reduction in surface area and pore volume as the same to bimetallic HKUST-1/Ru and HKUST-1/Zn [56, 57]. Gul-E-Noor et al. [50] reported the BET surface area of bimetallic HKUST-1/Zn-MOF was reduced by $27\%$ compared to pristine HKUST-1. While it was significantly reduced by $63\%$ in Cu-Ru-BTC MOF. In the present study, bimetallic MOF with addition of $5\%$ UiO-66 has almost $58\%$ decrease in surface area but the pores are enlarged to mesopores in C1 while $5\%$ addition of UiO-66 in HKUST-1 resulted in $44\%$ decrease in surface area $(Z1)$ . However, the reduction of surface area and pore volume didn’t disturb adsorptive performance of bimetallic MOF. The average pore diameter was calculated using the BJH method from desorption data and is given in Table 1. The pore diameter of bimetallic MOFs increased and micropores were transformed to mesopores. The hysteresis between adsorption-desorption isotherm of ${\bf N}_{2}$ at higher relative pressures depicts mesoporous nature of the materials [52]. The pore size distributions are shown in Fig. 6 and it is seen that a mesopore peak was appearing for binary MOFs (Fig.6).
## 3.2. Effect of pH on adsorption
It is very important to study the effect of initial $\mathsf{p H}$ as it has a strong effect on adsorption capacity. In this study, the pH range was selected in 3-12 at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with initial concentration of MB at 5 ppm (Fig. 7). Generally, the adsorption of MB increases with the $\mathsf{p H}$ increase, showing the significance of electrostatic interaction in the adsorptive removal of MB. However, in industrial processes, the $\mathrm{pH}$ of wastewater varies widely. For the MOFs in this work, significant removal of MB has been observed in all $\mathrm{pH}$ values between 3 and 12. The point of zero charge $\mathrm{(pH_{PZC})}$ was determined using the immersion technique [49]. The pHPZC values for C, Z, C1, and Z1 are $4.2{\pm}0.2\$ , $5.5{\pm}0.2\$ [7, 42], $5.0{\pm}0.2\$ and $4.0{\pm}0.2\$ , respectively (Fig. S5). The surface of MOFs becomes positively charged below their pHPZC, while negatively charged above pHPZC. The adsorption of MB is less at pH 3 while it starts increasing with higher pH. Since the $\mathrm{pH}_{\mathrm{PZC}}$ of MOFs is less than 6 so the surface of MOFs becomes negatively charged at pH higher than 6. Moreover, MB is a cationic dye which is adsorbed in a higher quantity due to electrostatic interactions. As seen, the maximum adsorption for all MOFs occurs at $\mathrm{pH}7.9$ . The initial pH for all further experiments was then selected at 7.5 in this study.
## 3.3. Adsorption kinetics of MB
All the samples were tested for the adsorption kinetics using a fixed amount of adsorbent and adsorbate concentrations for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The adsorption data were analyzed using pseudo first-order and second-order kinetic models (Fig. S1 and S2) [58, 59]. There is not a big
difference between experimental and calculated values of $\mathrm{Q_{e}}$ using pseudo $1^{\mathrm{st}}$ order kinetic model and pseudo $2^{\mathrm{nd}}$ order models. The values of ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{R}}}^{2}$ are higher in both cases. The pseudo $1^{\mathrm{st}}$ order and pseudo $2^{\mathrm{nd}}$ order kinetic models can be written as follows.
$$
\ln(Q_{e}-Q_{t})=\ln Q_{e}-k_{1}t
$$
$$
\frac{t}{{{Q}_{t}}}\mathrm{{=}}\frac{1}{{{k}_{2}}{{Q}^{2}}_{e}}\mathrm{+}\frac{1}{{{Q}_{e}}}t
$$
Where, $Q_{e}$ is the amount of MB adsorbed at equilibrium $(\mathrm{mg/g)}$ ; $\textstyle{\mathcal{Q}}_{t}^{}$ is the amount adsorbed at time t $\mathrm{(mg/g)}$ ; $t$ is the adsorption time in minutes. The calculated values of $k_{1}$ $\dot{\boldsymbol{\mathfrak{c}}}_{1},\boldsymbol{k}_{2},\boldsymbol{Q}_{e,\mathrm{exp}}$ and $Q_{e,c a l}$ are given in Table 2. In case of AC, all MB is removed in just first 5 min so it’s not shown here.
In general, as the surface of the adsorbent is covered with the adsorbate molecules, the tendency of the adsorbate molecules to get into and/or on the surface becomes low due to a less number of available sites for the adsorption [35]. The kinetic behaviors of all the MOFs are given in Fig. 8. The adsorption is faster on C compared to all other MOFs. The adsorption kinetics of C1 is also faster than Z and Z1 owing to both large pore size and surface area [34].
Generally, smaller pore sizes of adsorbents than MB molecules result in slow diffusion of MB molecules into the pores. However, the pore size of all studied MOFs is larger than the size of MB molecules facilitating higher adsorption of MB on C due to its larger pore compared to C1, $Z$ and Z1. Most of the adsorption is achieved in 150 min. However, most of the adsorption is achieved on C in the first $90\mathrm{min}$ . It can also be noted that the adsorption on Z1 is a gradual process and adsorption quantity gradually increases during 12 h of operation. In case of Z1, the data were obtained in regular intervals for all $12\mathrm{~h~}$ and it was fitted more accurately with pseudo $1^{\mathrm{st}}$ order kinetic model.
## 3.4. Adsorption isotherms and mechanism
The adsorption isotherms of MB are shown in Fig. 9. The adsorption data were fitted with two well-known models, i.e. Langmuir and Freundlich isotherms (Fig. S3 and S4) [60, 61]. The mathematical forms of both the models are given below.
Langmuir model: $\frac{C_{e}}{Q_{e}}=\frac{1}{K_{L}Q_{m}}+\frac{C_{e}}{Q_{e}}$
where $Q_{e}$ represents equilibrium dye concentration on an adsorbent $\mathrm{(mg/g)}$ , $C_{e}$ represents dye concentration in solution $(\mathrm{mg/L})$ at equilibrium, $Q_{{\scriptscriptstyle m}}$ is the monolayer capacity of dye adsorption $(\mathrm{mg/g)}$ , $K_{\iota}$ represents the Langmuir adsorption constant $\mathrm{{(L/mg)}}$ . The favorability of an adsorption system can also be expressed in terms of a dimensionless separation factor ( $R_{L}$ ) defined as follows.
$$
R_{L}=\frac{1}{1+C_{m}K_{L}}
$$
Where, $C_{m}$ is the maximum initial concentration of adsorbate. The adsorption process is favorable in the $0<\widehat{\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{L}}}<1$
$$
\mathrm{Freundlich\:model{:}\:\:l n}Q_{e}=\ln K_{{F}}+\frac{1}{n}\ln C_{e}
$$
where $\mathcal{Q}_{e}^{\phantom{\dagger}}$ represents equilibrium dye concentration on an adsorbent $(\mathrm{mg/g)}$ , $C_{e}$ represents dye concentration in solution $(\mathrm{mg/L})$ at equilibrium, $K_{r}$ and $\mathfrak{n}$ are the Freundlich constants, characteristics of the system.
The adsorption data were fitted more accurately with the Langmuir model compared to the Freundlich model based on high ${\bf R}^{2}$ values and the difference between the experimental and calculated values of maximum adsorption capacity. The values of Langmuir and Freundlich parameters are given in Table 3. The adsorption capacities of composite MOFs were higher than pristine HKUST-1 and UiO-66. The adsorption capacity is the highest on C1 compared to all other MOFs. The adsorption capacity is increased due to the synergic effects of UiO-66 and larger pore size of C1. The pristine UiO-66 i.e., Z, presents the adsorption of MB less than all other MOFs and AC with a capacity of $107~\mathrm{mg/g}$ . For the composite UiO-66 (Z1), the adsorption capacity is increased to $208~\mathrm{mg/g}$ , almost double of the adsorption capacity of Z. The increased adsorption capacity may result from the synergic effects of HKUST-1, which has a higher adsorption capacity in both pristine and composite forms. It is clear that the textural properties don’t have a large effect on adsorption for the studied MOFs. Previously, an effect of metal ions on the adsorption of MB, MO and MB/MO mixture has been reported elsewhere [32] on two identical surface area MOFs, MIL-100-Fe and MIL101-Cr. MIL-100-Fe was more efficient for dye adsorption compared to MIL-100-Cr.
The adsorption capacity of different adsorbents is presented in Table 4 for a comparison with the MOFs and AC studied in this study. The adsorption capacities of the HKUST-1 and its composite MOFs are higher than MWCNTs, MIL-100-Cr, HKUST-1, HKUST/GO and ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3},$ /carbon. However, adsorption capacities of MIL-101-Al and ZJU-24 are higher than both copper and zirconium MOFs presented in this study.
The adsorption of dyes is usually dependent on the electrostatic interactions, $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions, hydrogen bonding and hydrophobic interactions among adsorbents and the dyes. In a typical adsorption process, one or combination of all these mechanisms can occur [7, 29, 39, 62, 63]. In the present study, the adsorption of MB at pH range 3-12 is significantly high, which shows that some other factors play important roles in the MB removal from water, for example, $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction. The pHPZCs for all the MOFs are less than pH 6 (Fig. S5), which shows that the surface of the MOFs may be positively charged at pH less than 6 and negatively charged at $\mathrm{pH}$ higher than 6. MB is a cationic dye, so there should be minimum adsorption in $\mathsf{p H}$ range 3-6 due to electrostatic repulsion and maximum at pH of 10. The adsorption on C is slightly decreased with increasing pH values greater than 8, which might result in the formation of NaCl from $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ ions (NaOH was used for pH adjustment) and Clions from MB [7]. Furthermore, a marginal increase in the adsorption on $Z$ with increasing pH higher than 8 is observed. However, it is not possible to conclude that electrostatic interaction is the only mechanism for MB removal from pH study. The significance of other factors such as $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions can be revealed by FTIR (Fig. 1). The bands at ${\approx}1370~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ and $\approx1580~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ belong to $(\mathbf{C}{=}\mathbf{C})_{\nu}$ of benzene ring [39]. The FTIR bands are drifted after the adsorption of MB on MOFs, signifying the $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions of the benzene rings of MOFs and MB. In case of C, the bands from $1377~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ is shifted to $1365~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and the one at 1548 cm is shifted to $1580~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ .  Similarly, in case of C1, the bands at $1558~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ is shifted to $1582\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and the band at $1368~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ is moved to $1395~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . However, in case of $Z$ a small drift from $1388~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ to 1391 and $1561~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ to $1563~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ shows a minor effect of $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions. The bands at $1391~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ and $1574~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ shifted to $1384~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ and $1554~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , respectively on Z1. The presence of BTC and BDC into framework provides extra benzene rings which help in forming stronger $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions. Moreover, the availability of high electron density owing to the COO- group provides active sites to cationic MB molecules, resulting in a higher adsorption capacity [39]. These observations attest to the complex adsorption mechanism of MB on the MOFs.
## 4. Conclusions
In this study, pristine and composites of HKUST-1 and UiO-66 MOFs have been synthesized and tested for the adsorption of MB from wastewater. The binary MOFs have reduced surface area, larger pore size and high thermal stability compared to pristine MOFs verified by various characterization techniques. Generally, all the MOFs showed high adsorption capacity and in some cases higher than the commercial activated carbons. However, binary MOFs in particular, showed a high adsorption capacity of MB despite of reduced surface area compared to pristine MOFs. The kinetic study showed that fast kinetics was observed on MOFs and can be fitted with pseudo $1^{\mathrm{st}}$ and pseudo $2^{\mathrm{nd}}$ order models. The enhanced adsorption capacity of composite MOFs, highlights the synergetic effects of multi-MOF systems. The maximum adsorption capacities of composite MOFs, C1 and $Z1$ , are 526 and
$208~\mathrm{mg/g}$ , respectively. The effect of pH showed that electrostatic interactions play a key role in the adsorption process. However, other controlling factors are $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions. The robustness of synthesized MOFs over a wide range of $\mathsf{p H}$ makes them suitable for the adsorptive removal of MB and other contaminants.
